<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martín de Ursúa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1653–1715</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Martín de Ursúa y Arizmendi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martín de Ursúa was a Spanish Basque soldier and colonial official, governor of Yucatán. He built a road from Yucatán south into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> and on 13 March 1697 stormed Nojpetén, the island capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itza-people">Itza Maya</a>, with troops in a galley built on the lake. The conquest ended the last independent Maya kingdom. He was later governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, where he died.</p></div>
